.TH INTERGEN 1 "2026-04-21" "intergen 0.1.0" "InterGenOS System Administration"
.SH NAME
intergen \- AI assistant for InterGenOS
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B intergen
.I command
[\fIargs\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B intergen
is the command-line interface to the InterGen AI assistant. Questions
are sent to the running InterGen daemon over D-Bus. If the service is
not running, the command reports how to start it and exits non-zero.
An explicit
.B \-\-direct
permits an in-process session when no daemon owns the bus name; that
session ends with the command.
.PP
InterGen selects an LLM and quantisation appropriate to the local
hardware by detecting the system's CPU, RAM, and GPU at
.B intergen setup
time. The recommended model + quantisation and the hardware tier are
shown by
.BR "intergen tier" .
.PP
InterGen runs as a per-user systemd user service
.RB ( intergen.service )
with D-Bus activation on the session bus. Status and tier queries can
activate the service; asking a question requires it to be running unless
the explicit direct-session option is used. Start the managed service
with
.BR "systemctl --user start intergen.service" .
Run a foreground daemon explicitly with
.BR "intergen daemon" .
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BR ask " [\fB\-\-direct\fR] \fIMESSAGE\fR"
Ask InterGen a question or issue a natural-language instruction.
Returns the response on stdout. If no daemon is running, the command
reports the service state and exits non-zero. With
.BR \-\-direct ,
it may instead start a session in this process for the duration of the
call. A running daemon is used even when this option is supplied.
.TP
.B status
Show daemon status: whether it is running, version, hardware tier,
number of requests handled, last error, and which internal components
are ready.
.TP
.B tier
Show hardware-tier detection results: tier level, RAM, GPU vendor and
model, recommended model and quantisation, and estimated model size on
disk.
.TP
.B tools
List the CLI's built-in tools and their fixed schema safety tiers:
.PP
.nf
run_command        confirm
read_file          auto
write_file         confirm
manage_packages    confirm
manage_services    confirm
web_search         auto
open_application   auto
.fi
.PP
These are the declared tiers printed by this command; validation of an
individual action and operating-system authorization still apply.
This release connects no MCP server and reads neither
.I /etc/intergen/mcp.yml
nor
.IR /etc/intergen/mcp.d/ .
.TP
.BR "tool-log" " [\fB\-\-clear\fR|\fB\-\-json\fR|\fB\-\-count\fR] [\fB\-\-limit\fR \fIN\fR]"
Show or wipe the per-user D-008 dispatch audit log. The log records
every tool call InterGen made on the user's behalf, the declared and
effective provenance per the D-008 provenance gate, ingress tools that
fired earlier in the same conversation turn, the user's review-modal
decision, and the result summary. The log lives at
.IR $XDG_STATE_HOME/intergen/tool-dispatch.jsonl
(typically
.IR ~/.local/state/intergen/tool-dispatch.jsonl )
with mode 0600, and is owned by the user; the system never reads it.
Without arguments,
.B intergen tool-log
prints the last 50 records in human-readable form. With
.B \-\-limit
.I N
the limit changes to
.I N
records (must be >= 1). With
.B \-\-json
the output is the raw JSONL lines instead, suitable for piping into
.BR jq (1).
.B \-\-count
prints just the total record count and exits.
.B \-\-clear
truncates the log to zero bytes — the user-data-deletion path
documented in the project's privacy policy (see
.I PRIVACY.md
at the repository root) for the GDPR right-to-erasure. Truncation
preserves the file inode and 0600 permissions across wipe cycles. The
gate continues to function after a clear; subsequent dispatches start
a fresh log.
.TP
.B test
Run the in-tree self-test (hardware detection + tool subsystem) and
exit with status 0 on success or 1 on failure. Primarily useful after
a configuration change or an upgrade.
.TP
.BR setup " [\fB\-\-yes\fR]"
Download and verify the recommended LLM model for the detected tier and
the embedding model. With
.B \-\-yes
runs non-interactively. Models are stored under
.IR /var/lib/intergen/models/ .
.TP
.B daemon
Start the InterGen daemon in the foreground. Normally run by the
systemd user service, not directly.
.TP
.BR help ", " \-\-help ", " \-h
Print usage.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/intergen/config.yml
System-wide configuration, including the sampling parameters, logging
settings and optional memory DB path. Settings marked reserved are not
read by this release. In particular, the LLM endpoint is derived from
the serving port; the reserved endpoint and MCP settings do not change it
or the built-in tools' tiers.
.TP
.I ~/.config/intergen/config.yml
Per-user overrides applied on top of the system-wide configuration.
.TP
.I /etc/intergen/mcp.yml
Reserved location. This release does not read it and connects no MCP
server. It does not configure the built-in tools.
.TP
.I /etc/intergen/mcp.d/
Reserved directory, not read by this release. Files placed here do not
override the built-in tools' permissions or tiers.
.TP
.I /var/lib/intergen/models/
Local LLM and embedding models. Populated by
.BR "intergen setup" .
.TP
.I $XDG_DATA_HOME/intergen/memory.db
Default per-user memory database (typically
.IR ~/.local/share/intergen/memory.db ).
The
.B memory.db_path
configuration setting can select a different path accessible to the user.
.TP
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/intergen/intergen.log
Main log for the per-user service (typically
.IR ~/.local/state/intergen/intergen.log ).
The logging.file setting is read; a non-root process redirects a
configured path under /var or /usr to its XDG state directory.
The startup logging message names the actual destination.
.TP
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/intergen/events.jsonl
Structured event log for the per-user service (typically
.IR ~/.local/state/intergen/events.jsonl ).
Its path is resolved by the event logger; logging.event_log is reserved
and not read. logging.mcp_audit is also reserved and not read; this
release connects no MCP server and produces no MCP call audit trail.
.TP
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/intergen/tool-dispatch.jsonl
Per-user D-008 dispatch audit log (typically
.IR ~/.local/state/intergen/tool-dispatch.jsonl ).
Append-only JSONL recording every tool call's provenance and the user's
review-modal decision. User-owned, mode 0600. Rotated by
.IR /etc/logrotate.d/intergen-tool-dispatch
with a 30-day retention default. Read or wipe via
.BR "intergen tool-log" .
.SH EXAMPLES
Ask a question:
.PP
.RS
.B intergen ask "What desktop packages are installed?"
.RE
.PP
Check that the daemon is running and healthy:
.PP
.RS
.B intergen status
.RE
.PP
Confirm the hardware tier InterGen detected:
.PP
.RS
.B intergen tier
.RE
.PP
Download the recommended model non-interactively:
.PP
.RS
.B intergen setup \-\-yes
.RE
.PP
List callable tools and their safety tiers:
.PP
.RS
.B intergen tools
.RE
.SH SECURITY
Built-in tools declare their safety tiers in the shipped code. Tool
validation and operating-system authorization determine whether an
individual action can run; the reserved MCP paths do not configure
these decisions.
.PP
The escalation.mode configuration setting controls cloud-assistance
escalation, not the built-in tools' safety tiers. No MCP server is
connected by this release. The directory
.I /var/lib/intergen/mcp-pins/
is reserved; this release uses no MCP schema pins.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR pkm (1),
.BR systemctl (1),
.BR journalctl (1)
